  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem is fundamentally about minimizing the maximum overlap between a function and its shifted versions. The current strategy combines global exploration with local refinement. The central idea is to use a genetic algorithm with specification-based selection to find promising candidate solutions, followed by a coordinate descent phase that focuses on structural improvements.\n\nThis approach differs from the provided example by:\n- Using a genetic algorithm with specification-based fitness evaluation\n- Implementing competitive selection with explicit sizing increases\n- Breaking the genome strings to prevent premature convergence\n- Adding directional repair that maintains solution size during mutation\n- Using decoupled organization to manage complexity\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- **Representation**: 1D grid function with range [0,1] and normalization constraint\n- **Objective**: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over linear correlations\n- **Constraints**:\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - \u222bh(x)dx = 1 exactly\n- **Repair transformation**: When invalid solution found, introduce changes that maintain grid organization\n- **Optimization formulation**: Sum-based linear minimization with component-wise bounds\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Phase 1 (40% of budget): Genetic Algorithm\n   Inputs:\n     - Population size (n_parents \u2248 10)\n     - Mutation rate (rate \u2248 0.02)\n     - Selection pressure (tournament size \u2248 5)\n   Steps:\n     1. Initialize population:\n        - Copy parent solution if powered execution\n        - Otherwise generate random constrained solutions\n     2. Evaluation:\n        - Compute fitness = -float(max(correlation)) *dx (lower is better)\n        - Apply directional repair to invalid solutions\n     3. Selection:\n        - Use tournament selection with three challengers\n        - Prefer solutions reducing C5 at boundaries\n     4. Crossover:\n        - Perform uniform recombination with breakpoint selection\n     5. Mutation:\n        - Inject local modifications with direct repair\n        - Maintain grid structure during mutation\n\n   Phase 2 (60% of budget): Local Optimization\n   Inputs:\n     - Optimization steps (max_ops \u2248 500)\n     - Restart interval (restart_interval \u2248 30)\n   Steps:\n     1. Use coordinate descent on shuffled grid positions\n     2. Apply sliding window correction with size scaling\n     3. Perform targeted interval inversion\n     4. Repair after every 5 optimization steps\n     5. Evaluate C5 on violations and improve feasibility\n\n4. Search Choices:\n   - Population management:\n     - Tournament size: 5 \u00b12\n     - Mutation rate: 0.02*active_time\n     - Selection pressure: 0.75 preferred loyalty\n   - Optimization parameters:\n     - Max operations: 500 + 0.5*time_seconds\n     - Repair frequency: 30 iterations\n     - Correlation window: entire domain\n   - Competitive sizing:\n     - Grid breaks after 1000 operations\n     - Dynamic window adjustment\n     - Structure preservation during mutation\n\n5. Budget and Robustness:\n   - Core task: GA execution with repair\n   - Validation pass:\n     - Repair final solution first\n     - Recalculate normalized C5\n     - Verify integral constraint\n   - Progress monitoring:\n     - Time tracking per phase\n     - Target C5 threshold: 0.3808\n     -\u8f9c\u8d1f tolerance: C5 stabilized under 0.3808\n   - Competitive sizing:\n     - Initial population: 10 individuals\n     - Maximum population: 20 members\n     - Restart at 0.1% time thresholds\n   - Time estimates:\n     - GA phase: estimate 0.2-0.3s/operation (1-2 operations/second)\n     - Local phase: 0.05s/operation (6-10 operations/second)\n\n6. Validation Contract:\n   - Fitness evaluation:\n     - Compute correlation with mode='full'\n     - Apply window normalization by dx=2/n\n     - Take minimum normalized value\n   - Repair verification:\n     - Clip all entries to [0,1]\n     - Adjust sum to target using boundary selection\n     - Maintain solution structure using ROI repair\n   - Return protocols:\n     - Fixed point positions\n     - Normalized C5 value\n     - Guaranteed grid size\n   - Competitive sizing:\n     - Grid breaks at 1000 operations\n     - Progressive window splitting\n     - Structure migration avoidance",
